  "slug": "structure-fast-6329",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications apply protein language models and residue-level graphs to predict mutational and enzymatic property changes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is combining multiple representations or site-aware modules rather than relying on a single sequence embedding. MPFIL integrates structural and sequence features of wild-type and mutant complexes to predict mutation-induced binding affinity changes, and ECL-Net couples protein language model embeddings with residue interaction graphs, EGNN, and contrastive learning for thermostability. SAEKP adds a site attention module that reweights enzyme and substrate representations to predict kcat, Km, and Ki. AltereGO fuses score-level predictions from four protein representations for Gene Ontology molecular function, and KINBERT pairs a protein language model with phospho-PCA screening and Bayesian hierarchical modeling for kinase-substrate mapping. Other items diverge: AdventML predicts enzyme optimal temperature from transformer embeddings with resampling, ESMRank applies learning-to-rank to deep mutational scans, and MIL-BC treats proteins as bags of automatically detected domains. These items do not share one method, so the common element is multimodal or context-aware representation design.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-31"
}
